  own SUPPORTED verdict.
  Version 0.46.0
  (2026-07-30) continued the v3 backfill with Academy of Management Journal
  volume 65 issue 1 and volume 64 issue 6 — 26 notes, all v2 augmentations.
  A backfill adds no papers, so the record total is unchanged at 1,167; the
  version-tier census shifts to 61 v1, 784 v2, and 322 v3 notes. The upgrade
  phase was the third consecutive clean one, with all 26 augmentations passing
  the validator and the mechanical diff-guard on the first attempt and no
  self-fix cycles, and each touched note passed a fresh full independent
  nine-field rubric-v2 audit at 229/234 prose-field verdicts SUPPORTED with
  zero UNSUPPORTED or CONTRADICTED, after twenty-one evidence-based repairs
  across sixteen notes in three repair rounds — the heaviest repair load of the
  backfill to date. Fifteen of the twenty-one repairs fell on legacy prose
  fields written before the v3 standard, and practical-implication scope drift
  was the dominant class with six instances, three of them on papers whose text
  contains no practical-implications section at all and none of the audiences
  the notes addressed. The multi-study support-pattern class recurred for a
  third consecutive batch, and one note inverted a premise outright, resting
  causal inference on an instrumental-variable model the paper concludes is
  unnecessary. Six repairs touched new v3 fields, five of them Data & Measures
  with five distinct causes, none a repeated defect. Five residual PARTIAL
  verdicts are documented rather than edited: two are the interleaved-references
  truncation class, in which a two-column REFERENCES heading sits above
  continuing Future Research prose so the strip discards it and the flagged
  claims verify verbatim in the raw text; two rest on an auditor locality error
  and a framing nuance whose content the auditor conceded is true; and one sits
  atop a self-contradictory figure description, where the note follows the
  paper's five separate statements of the effect's direction rather than the
  ambiguous caption. The backfill continued to function as a corpus audit of
  pre-v3 prose: in every case where a legacy field and a newly written v3
  section covered the same fact, the new section was right. Version 0.45.0
  (2026-07-30) continued the v3 backfill with Academy of Management Journal
  volume 65 issues 3 and 2 — 26 notes, all v2 augmentations. A backfill adds
  no papers, so the record total is unchanged at 1,167; the version-tier
  census shifts to 61 v1, 810 v2, and 296 v3 notes. All 26 augmentations
  passed the validator and the mechanical diff-guard on the first attempt with
  no self-fix cycles, and each touched note passed a fresh full independent
  nine-field rubric-v2 audit at 232/234 prose-field verdicts SUPPORTED with
  zero UNSUPPORTED or CONTRADICTED, after nine evidence-based repairs across
  seven notes in three repair rounds. Issue 2 was the cleanest issue of the
  backfill to date at 116/117 SUPPORTED. Seven of the nine repairs fell on
  legacy prose fields written before the v3 standard, two of them recurrences
  of the documented multi-study support-pattern overstatement (an indirect
  effect said to hold across four studies where one never measured the
  mediator; six hypotheses said to be supported where the paper calls one
  partial), and one an inverted generalizability premise on a paper that argues
  for its setting's transferability. Two repairs touched new v3 fields, with
  unrelated causes. Two residual PARTIAL verdicts are documented rather than
  edited: both are verification artifacts of the interleaved-references
  truncation class, in which a two-column REFERENCES heading sits above
  continuing Limitations and Future Research prose so the strip discards it,
  and both notes' flagged claims verify verbatim in the raw extracted text.
  Version 0.44.0
  (2026-07-30) continued the v3 backfill with Academy of Management Journal
  volume 65 issues 5 and 4 — 25 notes, all v2 augmentations. A backfill adds
  no papers, so the record total is unchanged at 1,167; the version-tier
  census shifts to 61 v1, 836 v2, and 270 v3 notes. The upgrade phase was the
  cleanest of the backfill so far, with all 25 augmentations passing the
  validator and the mechanical diff-guard on the first attempt and no self-fix
  cycles. Each touched note passed a fresh full independent nine-field
  rubric-v2 audit at 218/225 prose-field verdicts SUPPORTED with zero
  UNSUPPORTED or CONTRADICTED, after fifteen evidence-based repairs across
  thirteen notes in two repair rounds. Thirteen of the fifteen fell on legacy
  prose fields written before the v3 standard. Both documented Mechanism
  Process failure classes appeared again — one note narrated a disconfirmed
  hypothesis as the paper's prediction (Matusik), and two misstated how a
  model is wired (Solomon, Zhang) — while scope drift on Practical
  Implication was the dominant legacy pattern at four instances, each
  addressing prescriptions to audiences the paper never names. Two repairs
  touched new v3 fields, with unrelated causes. Seven residual PARTIAL
  verdicts are documented rather than edited: three are verification
  artifacts from a newly identified truncation class in which a two-column
  REFERENCES heading sits above continuing Discussion prose, so the strip
  discards the interleaved tail; one reproduces a paper's own internal
  discrepancy faithfully; and three are framing nuances on legacy fields
  accepted under the repair convergence bound. In every case where a legacy
  field and a newly written v3 section covered the same fact, the new section
  was correct and the legacy field wrong, so the backfill is also functioning
  as a corpus audit of pre-v3 prose. Version 0.43.0
  (2026-07-29) continued the v3 backfill with Academy of Management Journal
  volume 66 issue 1 and volume 65 issue 6 — 26 notes, all v2 augmentations and
  the largest batch of the backfill so far. A backfill adds no papers, so the
  record total is unchanged at 1,167; the version-tier census shifts to 61 v1,
  861 v2, and 245 v3 notes. Each touched note passed a fresh full independent
  nine-field rubric-v2 audit at 228/234 prose-field verdicts SUPPORTED with
  zero UNSUPPORTED or CONTRADICTED, after thirteen evidence-based repairs
  across eight notes in three repair rounds. Twelve of the thirteen fell on
  legacy prose fields written before the v3 standard, and this is the first
  batch in which both documented Mechanism Process failure classes appeared
  together: three notes misstated what the paper predicted — a disconfirmed
  conditionality narrated as theory (Dimotakis), an explicitly unhypothesized
  claim presented as a prediction (Ong), and a downturn called partially borne
  out that occurred in neither study (Xu) — while two misstated how a model is
  wired (Chan's value-moderating practices, Dwertmann's causal reasoning and
  motive moderation). One repair touched a new v3 field, where recruited-sample
  demographics had been attached to post-exclusion analysed Ns across three
  experiments (Kundro). A second round caught a generalizability caveat the
  paper itself rejects, and a third round found the same premise had propagated
  to an adjacent prose field — a new pattern, since repairing one field left
  the other wrong. Six PARTIAL verdicts are accepted and documented rather than
  edited, because editing accurate content to satisfy a verification artifact
  would itself be a faithfulness failure: three rest on appendices the audit
  tooling trims for context budget, one draws anchored components from a paper
  that states no practical implications, and two are re-scorings of nuances
  that two earlier independent audit rounds had noted and passed. This batch
  ran on claude-opus-5. Earlier, version 0.42.0
  (2026-07-28) continued the v3 backfill with Academy of Management Journal
